usamts-2006-r3-04.body:
Suppose $n - k + 1$ players ("good" players) each score at least $n + k - 1$, and call the other $k - 1$ players "bad". The good players need at least $(n-k+1)(n+k-1)$ points in total. But
- games between two good players give them $2\binom{n-k+1}{2} = (n-k+1)(n-k)$ points;
- games between a good and a bad player give them at most $2(n-k+1)(k-1)$ points;
- games between two bad players give them nothing.

That is at most $(n-k+1)(n-k+2k-2) = (n-k+1)(n+k-2)$ points, fewer than needed. So at most $n - k$ players score $n + k - 1$ or more. If Alice scores $m \ge n + k - 1$, at most $n - k - 1$ other players have $m$ or more points, so at least $k$ players score fewer than her.

The construction in the first approach (good players tie each other and beat the bad players, then Alice's score is lowered one point at a time) shows that no $m \le n + k - 2$ works. For $n = 12$, $k = 8$ the threshold is $19$: with $18$ points, Alice could be in a group of five players who tie among themselves and beat the other seven, leaving her ahead of only $7$ players.
